<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kulmbach</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kulmbach is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, overlooked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plassenburg">Plassenburg</a> fortress. It passed to the Hohenzollern burgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1340 and was the seat of the margraviate of Brandenburg-Kulmbach until the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> in the early 17th century. It is known for its long brewing tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kulmbach.jpg" alt="Kulmbach" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reinhold Möller Ermell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulmbach_Luftbild-20240907-RM-160517.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The burgraves held the castle, rights of justice and lands around Nuremberg, and gradually built a territory in Franconia, acquiring Ansbach in 1331 and <b>Kulmbach</b> and Bayreuth.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Hohenzollern rulers of the Burgraviate of Nuremberg divided their Franconian lands in 1398, the territory 'above the mountains' around <b>Kulmbach</b> and the Plassenburg went to John III.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Principality of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</a></li></ul></section>
